Purpose: Document divergences found and fixed

[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergences found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every row above now passes against both `.so`s. Two genuine C↔Rust divergences
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m were found while building the error-path tests; both were in the same class and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both were fixed on the Rust side.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | row | C result | Rust result (before fix) | fix |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|-----|----------|--------------------------|-----|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1 | 31 (`stralloc` with `remaining > 0`, `storage == NULL`) | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `translation/Cargo.toml`: `debug-assertions = false` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 2 | 34 (`stralloc` with `a->block ≥ 100`, `realloc` returning `NULL`) | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | same |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Root cause: rustc inserts null-pointer-dereference debug assertions when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `debug-assertions` is on. Where the C code simply faults on a bad address, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `dev`-profile Rust build panicked and (with `panic = "abort"`) raised `SIGABRT`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead of `SIGSEGV`. The `release` profile already matched the C, because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `debug-assertions` defaults to off there. Turning the checks off in **both**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m profiles — for the same reason the crate already sets `overflow-checks = false`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m — makes the dev and release builds behave identically to the C library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Behaviours that look like bugs but are faithful to the C and are therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m asserted as-is rather than "fixed":
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `stbds_hmput_key` not refreshing `stbds_temp_key` in its wrapped probe scan,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   which makes `stbds_shputs` double-free on duplicate keys (row 30b).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `stbds_hmdel_key` tripping `STBDS_ASSERT(slot >= 0)` at lib.c:846 whenever
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `mode >= 2` and the deleted element is not the last one (covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `phase_b_delete_arena::row_40b_delete_ptr_to_string_mode_asserts`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `stbds_siphash_bytes` sign-extending `d[3] << 24` into the upper half of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `data` (rows 40, 41).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `stbds_hash_bytes` ignoring its `seed` entirely, because each `v0..v3`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   initialiser XORs it in twice (CONFIGS row 11).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `printf("%s %d\n", strmap[z], strmap[z].value)` in `str_dups` passing a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   16-byte struct by value to a variadic function; under the SysV AMD64 ABI
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%s` consumes `.key` and `%d` consumes `.value` (CONFIGS rows 52, 53).
